1
00:00:00,142 --> 00:00:01,980
How does Google build the future?

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:07,400
Inside Google sits a group of researchers dedicated to exploring the art of the possible.

3
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:15,760
And their mission is to drive the transformative breakthroughs that not only transform Google's products, but help solve some of humanity's biggest challenges.

4
00:00:16,494 --> 00:00:32,540
From the team that invented the transformer, and this is the architecture that sparked the generative AI era, to quantum computing, to early warning systems for floods, to AI that accelerates scientific discovery, Google Research is pushing the boundaries of what is possible in science and technology.

5
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:38,500
Today, I'm sitting down with the person leading all of these diverse efforts at Google Research, Yossi Mattias.

6
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:46,660
Now, not only is Yossi a prolific researcher, I mean, he won the Google Prize for the seminal algorithm that allowed AI to scale, he's also a builder.

7
00:00:47,182 --> 00:00:55,020
You've likely experienced his work without even knowing it, from Autocomplete to Google Trends to various Google search experiences used by billions of people.

8
00:00:56,046 --> 00:01:01,020
Today, I want to ask him, what feels impossible right now that won't be for long?

9
00:01:01,710 --> 00:01:04,599
I'm Sinead Bovell, and this is the Google Research Podcast.

10
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,560
Yossi, welcome to the podcast.

11
00:01:13,102 --> 00:01:14,020
Nice to be here, Sinead.

12
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:24,560
I think to start, to me, Google Research, it feels like one of the places inside Google where the future gets prototyped before it becomes a part of everyday life.

13
00:01:25,038 --> 00:01:27,420
How would you describe what Google Research does?

14
00:01:27,886 --> 00:01:40,720
Sure, let me start by saying this is really the golden age of research because I don't think ever before could we actually imagine the future and help bring it to reality in the pace and the scope that we can do today.

15
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:56,140
Now Google Research, you know, Google actually started with a research paper and Google Research has been always the organization or the team where we looked into how to bring and address problems that would matter.

16
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:59,720
Quite often, it's actually making the impossible possible.

17
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:08,080
The scope is pretty broad because we care a lot about a variety of areas that would make a difference.

18
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:42,884
Anything from how to advance machine learning, foundational and applied machine learning, to how to develop new algorithms, to solve a host of problems, how to build computing systems that we could actually use into the future, and how to use AI to help address problems in healthcare, on the climate, on education, how to drive science, how to accelerate science, and also how to advance the generative AI foundations, which are, of course, now, are really revolutionizing everything that we do around us.

19
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:47,300
And also looking to other paradigms, for example, building a quantum computer.

20
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:53,560
I was watching one of your presentations, and there were two slides that showed the breadth of work that you cover.

21
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:57,320
So, one showed foundational machine learning and algorithms all the way to quantum.

22
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:01,680
And then the next slide, it showed from the planet down to the cell.

23
00:03:01,966 --> 00:03:03,680
So, it's really the whole spectrum.

24
00:03:04,558 --> 00:03:13,940
But you have this unique ability to see what is going to happen in the future or to go into the future, but also be building it at the same time.

25
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:16,120
And you've consistently been right.

26
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:20,040
So, we're all chatting to AI systems today as if it's the complete normal thing to do.

27
00:03:20,046 --> 00:03:24,800
So eight years ago, you built Google Duplex, which is one of the first conversational AI systems.

28
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:30,400
Google Research is where one of the most infamous papers in modern AI dropped.

29
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:33,460
Attention is All You Need, which introduced the world to the transformer.

30
00:03:34,158 --> 00:03:39,440
So how do you see where things are going to go while simultaneously building it?

31
00:03:39,822 --> 00:03:42,640
And of course, I have to ask, what's about to happen next?

32
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:51,040
Interestingly enough, when we think about technology development, then it's not about extrapolation from what we've done so far.

33
00:03:51,246 --> 00:03:53,740
The beauty is about anticipating what's coming next.

34
00:03:54,420 --> 00:04:13,820
And really importantly, what we're doing at Google Research is really to look into not only into what we can do in the next three months, six months, nine months, but also what's in the horizon, what's around the corner, what are things that are actually going to become possible and we can actually make them possible where we are.

35
00:04:14,254 --> 00:04:24,260
So we have this interesting dynamics that in any given moment, And whatever breakthrough that we have and advancement, this is becoming a layer on which we can actually take the next level.

36
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:30,300
Going back to your example about conversational AI and other technologies.

37
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:43,980
So at some point, it's about identifying where the trends are going and having a judgment call about what is it that we are already on the verge of being able to do, even if we don't know yet how to do that.

38
00:04:44,430 --> 00:04:49,080
For example, a decade ago, it became clear to me that conversational experience is going to be…

39
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:58,620
We're on the verge of being able to do that because we started seeing that speech processing was better, that we could start seeing initial technologies for textual speech, and, of course, we take it for granted.

40
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:05,060
It's clear that having conversational interaction, that's the ultimate user interface.

41
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:06,860
That's how we communicate with each other.

42
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:17,220
Since then, one thing that I was particularly excited about is the question of how can we actually use AI and develop these technologies that are going to enable us this conversational experience.

43
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:32,940
Duplex was such an example, of course, and by now there have been just even a couple years ago we already shared that there were more than one trillion views of business information made possible by duplex technology calling businesses to ask about opening hours and so forth.

44
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:36,460
But it's not only about conversational experience.

45
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:39,520
It's also about how to remove barriers of modalities.

46
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:55,440
So, for example, how to convert content between text to images to videos, how to enable communication in multi-modal and across languages to remove language barriers, how to actually consume content in different ways.

47
00:05:56,142 --> 00:05:58,400
And again, now we're getting it for granted.

48
00:05:58,798 --> 00:06:09,960
So being at Google, and I've been at Google in search actually for over a decade, people got used to the fact that if you look for information, just Google it.

49
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:12,760
Similarly, we get used to other technologies.

50
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:15,780
We just assume that they're working once they do.

51
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:18,900
I like to call it ambient intelligence.

52
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:28,620
That's actually where the intelligence is becoming so good that you just take it for granted and you don't worry about it, and you take on to the next thing.

53
00:06:29,166 --> 00:06:39,440
I think we're very, you know, there's this saying that advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic, which I think is very true.

54
00:06:39,886 --> 00:06:49,380
But what is also true is that once we experience it a few times, it's not only no longer magic, we actually assume it's just happening.

55
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:50,860
We just assume it works.

56
00:06:51,438 --> 00:06:53,764
And then we can look into the next thing.

57
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:54,600
Right.

58
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:55,980
So, and I think it's Arthur C.

59
00:06:56,060 --> 00:06:56,540
Clarke, right?

60
00:06:56,540 --> 00:06:59,880
that any advanced technology is also indistinguishable from magic.

61
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:00,960
And it's so true, right?

62
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:09,200
Because the things that we are all doing today with AI, a few years ago, would have seemed extraordinary or impossible or completely radical.

63
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:10,640
And now we're all just streaming it.

64
00:07:11,086 --> 00:07:14,460
So in terms of where you see us going, you said two things.

65
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:17,460
So one, I think you're pointing to generative UI.

66
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:20,940
And I read the paper that you published with co-authors in February.

67
00:07:21,486 --> 00:07:24,260
And then the second was ambient intelligence.

68
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:30,420
So, the vision where we could head towards is a world where we're just almost streaming artificial intelligence.

69
00:07:30,702 --> 00:07:33,140
The way we stream electricity and nobody's thinking about it, right?

70
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:35,760
Nobody is thinking, oh, is my cell phone going to charge?

71
00:07:36,014 --> 00:07:36,980
You assume it's there.

72
00:07:37,358 --> 00:07:46,100
It's an ambient vision, one where you just stream and you assume you can ask the question that requires advanced intelligence without thinking about that, what's going into it.

73
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:47,280
Right.

74
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:50,436
So, you know, it's really about the art of the possible.

75
00:07:50,460 --> 00:07:55,680
Now, when you think about what does it mean to work on the RWA as possible, it's a combination of two things.

76
00:07:56,270 --> 00:07:58,800
What matters and what you can actually achieve.

77
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:11,428
Generative UI is a great example where an amazing team actually looked into what can we do now with generative UI to actually try new experiences for us.

78
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:22,560
And finding that, you know what, with generative UI, we cannot only generate the content that is magically kind of suitable for your prompt.

79
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:26,920
But you can also use it in order to figure out what is the best way to present it to you.

80
00:08:27,630 --> 00:08:32,179
If you're asking about, you know, something visual, show an image.

81
00:08:32,686 --> 00:08:37,200
If you're asking about an algorithm, then perhaps write a code and show me simulation.

82
00:08:37,710 --> 00:08:43,960
If you ask about fractal, writing the code for fractals and actually showing you the different fractals you can actually play with.

83
00:08:44,430 --> 00:09:01,020
If you're asking about what's the connection, what is quadratic equation, Your kid, perhaps, actually identified that the best way to do that is to show you a person shooting hoops and show you the connection of the angle in which you're shooting in the quadratic equation.

84
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:02,920
These are examples.

85
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:18,080
The beauty is that with AI actually accelerating the research itself as well as its deployment, we could see how from a demo it became now kind of a feature within search and Gemini app to actually give you a more engaging presentation.

86
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:18,640
Right.

87
00:09:19,374 --> 00:09:27,380
To me, this seems like a paradigm shift in how we interact with digital information, because it's no longer just the information being shown to you.

88
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:33,080
It's shown in a way that is relevant to you and to your context and maybe even your device.

89
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:39,160
And we were chatting earlier, and I was thinking, for some people, when they search a restaurant, they want to read the menu.

90
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:40,560
They are well-versed in food.

91
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:42,020
They know all the names of all the meals.

92
00:09:42,094 --> 00:09:44,120
But for other people, it's the picture that matters.

93
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:50,800
And so, eventually getting to a place where that interface, it shows the picture for the person that it knows, okay, this person is an image first person.

94
00:09:51,150 --> 00:09:55,940
And then for somebody who likes the fine names of food, they would just get the list of the text.

95
00:09:56,300 --> 00:09:59,780
And that's where things are, that's what's possible today, and that's where things are headed with GenUI.

96
00:10:00,750 --> 00:10:00,960
Right.

97
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:12,260
And taking the same capabilities in different domains, so GenUI, of course, turned out to be extremely powerful because with a single prompt, it could actually generate, and that's why we could actually put it in search.

98
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:25,340
But when thinking about the broader question about how do we actually want a presentation to be and how do we want to adapt to the user, not only intent but context, there are other areas in which are worth exploration.

99
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:30,720
For example, we started asking ourselves, can we be reimagining textbooks in education?

100
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:45,380
For example, if you want to study about gravity, rather than just reading a text with a few images, can we use AI in order to reimagine that and perhaps show examples, re-level it to the language that is suitable for the person?

101
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:48,840
It's different for a 10-year-old, for an 18-year-old, et cetera.

102
00:10:49,486 --> 00:10:51,492
Can we also adapt it to the interests?

103
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:58,120
If you like soccer and you're a 10-year-old girl, perhaps we can actually show you gravity with examples from that.

104
00:10:58,574 --> 00:11:04,720
So, in fact, there's this experiment we call Learn Your Way, which does exactly that and tries to reimagine.

105
00:11:05,060 --> 00:11:10,700
And, again, I think we're early on in actually using technology in order to, again, it starts with a problem.

106
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:12,320
What is it that we're solving for?

107
00:11:12,846 --> 00:11:14,580
And what can we do with technology today?

108
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:18,820
And what can we build that is going to solve even better into the future?

109
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:19,460
Right.

110
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:22,800
Can we do better than the static interface, the static standard textbook?

111
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:24,400
We all learn in different ways.

112
00:11:24,462 --> 00:11:28,640
and can the system eventually just understand the best use case for us?

113
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:28,960
Right.

114
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:43,420
Now, if we were to step back and think, what needs to happen from a technical standpoint for this vision of eventually the ambient intelligence layer where we're all just streaming AI, we don't think about it, where it's Gen UI, the user interface meets you where you are?

115
00:11:43,886 --> 00:11:45,840
From a technical standpoint, what needs to happen?

116
00:11:45,880 --> 00:12:04,820
Because we hear the industry talk a lot about scaling laws, we're coming up against the limits of pre-training, we maybe can't expect us to throw larger data sets, more compute, and see progress, but Google Research is specifically tasked with coming up with new architectures or driving those efficiency breakthroughs that could bypass some of those limits.

117
00:12:05,358 --> 00:12:09,460
So, what needs to happen architecturally, or do we need a different architecture to get to that vision?

118
00:12:10,638 --> 00:12:17,500
Yeah, so there's a lot of discussion about, okay, so where is the limit of what we do today, and did we reach that, etc.

119
00:12:18,350 --> 00:12:24,240
And in a way, there's almost disregard to the fact that, hey, just a few years ago, we didn't even have this architecture.

120
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:28,020
So why do we need to only think that this is the only way possible?

121
00:12:28,460 --> 00:12:34,660
And again, if I go to first principles of how do we drive research breakthroughs to solve problems.

122
00:12:34,780 --> 00:12:40,220
And when you think about generative AI, there are multiple areas that need more work and more research.

123
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:46,460
And we're looking into how to make them, for example, obviously making them more efficient is an obvious one.

124
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:50,160
Also making them more factual, more trustworthy.

125
00:12:50,222 --> 00:12:54,680
By the way, this is research we've been driving, we've been driven since 2021.

126
00:12:55,598 --> 00:13:11,720
So when the earth of LLMs, if you will, and even published a benchmark as early as 2022 to actually have as a benchmark for the, for academic community to actually look into how to test the factuality and consistency of LLMs.

127
00:13:12,462 --> 00:13:15,220
There are other areas that we need to make progress on.

128
00:13:15,220 --> 00:13:26,200
For example, it's not only about how to make reasoning better, how to solve harder problems, how to work in multi-dimensions, multi-modalities, and so forth.

129
00:13:26,574 --> 00:13:32,220
Now, we've seen huge progress on all of them, but in a way, every time you have this progress, this is the benchmark.

130
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:35,000
Now, the question is, where do you take it from here?

131
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:41,240
And you're always short of what you'd like to achieve because there's a bigger problem to achieve.

132
00:13:41,320 --> 00:13:43,060
You'd like to rely on that for more things.

133
00:13:43,060 --> 00:13:45,980
Now, here's an example on efficiency, for example.

134
00:13:48,206 --> 00:13:55,080
The scaling law assumed that you're only using current techniques and you just pump in more data and compute and so forth.

135
00:13:55,438 --> 00:13:57,740
But of course, there are new techniques, new algorithms.

136
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,240
The transformer was a new architecture, right?

137
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:23,020
Or just a couple of years ago, we came up with what we call speculative decoding, which essentially is a better algorithm for large LLM inference, showing that we can actually, —we have the inference with twice or more of efficiency, both in terms of time to inference, as well as how much compute is needed, without any loss of quality.

138
00:14:23,566 --> 00:14:24,880
And there are many variations.

139
00:14:25,020 --> 00:14:27,940
You can actually have much more than 2x.

140
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:34,360
And now there are hundreds of variations based on this paradigm, and now it's taken for granted that you can actually accelerate.

141
00:14:35,726 --> 00:14:52,800
But I'm actually waiting for additional innovations to see the 10x, the 100x, the new architectures, The, I mean, the fascinating thing about research is that it's not about how do you stretch what you already know into the best that you can on that.

142
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:57,920
Obviously that's something you always do when you develop technologies, but it's also coming up with these new approaches.

143
00:14:58,766 --> 00:15:11,740
It's never, I have yet to see something that is really a proof, here's something we cannot, that cannot be done, because it's only a question of when we can make this next breakthrough, the next innovation, the next turning point.

144
00:15:12,270 --> 00:15:18,500
So, on the one hand, there's still more gains to be, there's still more to do within the current architecture, right?

145
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:19,460
We can make them more efficient.

146
00:15:19,918 --> 00:15:21,100
We can make them more factual.

147
00:15:21,934 --> 00:15:25,860
And then simultaneously, there's breakthroughs that are still going to happen.

148
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:27,260
This isn't the end of the story.

149
00:15:27,380 --> 00:15:30,500
So, we're going to see innovations in the architectures themselves.

150
00:15:30,926 --> 00:15:35,700
And that's where you've said maybe 10x, 100x, a thousand times better than what we have today.

151
00:15:36,526 --> 00:15:36,800
Right.

152
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:41,500
And it's also, you know, So, what we're trying to do with technology is changing all the time.

153
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:44,740
We're actually raising the bar of what is it that we'd like to do.

154
00:15:45,260 --> 00:15:47,180
For example, how to apply to other places.

155
00:15:47,480 --> 00:15:52,620
Obviously, we started with language models, but obviously we'd like now to have models about the world.

156
00:15:53,166 --> 00:15:54,300
We'd like to have world models.

157
00:15:54,960 --> 00:16:03,920
We found out that in many cases actually by using foundational models, just by learning from the experiences, we get a lot of mileage.

158
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:16,220
But if we combine that also with various physics simulations, with understanding of additional material, we're going to get more insights, more training data.

159
00:16:16,260 --> 00:16:27,700
For example, in the molecular level, our biological data will give us actually even better models to actually, you know, ask questions about areas that today we are unable to ask those questions.

160
00:16:28,270 --> 00:16:38,340
So, or how to actually represent the relationship between various facts in the world and do that, actually build layers on top of that.

161
00:16:38,766 --> 00:16:41,920
And of course, use AI in order to help us even make that progress.

162
00:16:43,054 --> 00:16:56,920
So in some ways, do you think we're still kind of early in artificial intelligence, even though this field has been and it's existed for decades, which a lot of people may not know, but we're still so early on in what's going to become possible in this field?

163
00:16:57,742 --> 00:17:06,119
Yeah, I think it's this interesting dichotomy that on the one hand we say, oh, wait a minute, We're so far along than where we've been just a few years ago.

164
00:17:06,766 --> 00:17:14,859
On the other hand, it's pretty difficult to predict where we're going to be heading, because progress, you know, now we're talking about accelerating with AI itself.

165
00:17:15,662 --> 00:17:20,060
So AI is accelerating research, AI is accelerating the development of AI.

166
00:17:20,493 --> 00:17:26,740
Of course, people are talking at the point where it's going to be, you know, recursive improvement of AI and so forth.

167
00:17:27,150 --> 00:17:30,260
We already are using AI to accelerate research itself.

168
00:17:30,286 --> 00:17:39,480
So, yeah, in many ways, we are early on, and, you know, there's the saying, prediction is hard, especially about the future.

169
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:53,620
So, even though we are in the business of actually taking those bets, investing in things, I don't think anybody can really say with high confidence how the world is going to look like and how AI and technology is going to look like years from now.

170
00:17:54,062 --> 00:17:59,220
But one thing is clear, we are still making huge progress, and it's going to have impact on all aspects.

171
00:17:59,278 --> 00:18:16,820
And part of really what we're trying to do is to ask the right questions about where is it that we can have the biggest impact on making research breakthrough that is going to translate into impact on everything from products for core technologies, science, society, and so forth.

172
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:21,360
And let's talk about science because I know science is a big part of Google Research's work.

173
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:24,300
What does this moment mean for science?

174
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:25,560
What just happened to science?

175
00:18:25,838 --> 00:18:31,840
To me, one of the most exciting developments that we have now is how we accelerate scientific discovery itself.

176
00:18:32,558 --> 00:18:42,420
Not only how we use the best technologies that we have in order to ask concrete questions, but how to actually use AI to provide the kind of tools for the entire scientific methods.

177
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:47,920
If you think about what does science entail, think about the scientific lab.

178
00:18:48,526 --> 00:18:58,220
You have actually researchers working on trying to solve a question, such as what is the reason for certain bacteria to be more infectious than others?

179
00:18:58,926 --> 00:19:00,540
And why is it resistant to antibiotics?

180
00:19:01,560 --> 00:19:06,820
Which is an example that one of our partners from Imperial College actually were studying for years.

181
00:19:07,566 --> 00:19:17,360
And then you have actually researchers, quite often, researchers on all ranks, including also grad students and postdocs, actually looking into hypothesis.

182
00:19:18,222 --> 00:19:20,980
First, of course, looking at the literature, what can we learn from that?

183
00:19:21,060 --> 00:19:23,420
Looking into hypothesis, then testing them.

184
00:19:23,886 --> 00:19:27,780
Now, what we're looking at is, how can we use AI to accelerate all of that?

185
00:19:28,180 --> 00:19:41,540
For example, we have this system called AI Co-Scientist, which is a multi-agent system that essentially can do this literature search across all the literature, and then it can actually generate hypotheses.

186
00:19:42,880 --> 00:19:53,180
and it can generate many hypotheses, by the way, and use and try to validate these hypotheses and rank them and bring them back to the researcher and say, "Okay.

187
00:19:53,200 --> 00:19:59,000
here's an hypothesis, which may be answering that question, research question that you have in mind.

188
00:19:59,470 --> 00:20:11,800
And because the ability to look through the entire literature, and check the hypothesis versus whatever data that we have out there, it can actually accelerate research in a pretty tremendous way.

189
00:20:12,520 --> 00:20:22,780
In this particular example, by the way, the eco scientist came up with a hypothesis that took years for our partners to come up with in just three days.

190
00:20:23,374 --> 00:20:24,900
And it came up with another hypothesis.

191
00:20:25,646 --> 00:20:35,340
And we had other partners in Stanford and other places with AI co-scientists helping with hypothesis about liver fibrosis, about drug repurposing.

192
00:20:36,206 --> 00:20:45,480
So what we see here is that suddenly we can have an AI system that can serve the purpose of kind of a very powerful collaborator.

193
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:52,420
In fact, when I spoke with our partners, how was your experience working with that, it's like working with extremely capable collaborators.

194
00:20:53,580 --> 00:21:01,940
And interestingly enough, this is not just a smart collaborator, because it can actually read through literature and all the discipline.

195
00:21:02,542 --> 00:21:08,020
So it's essentially like having a collaborator who's a polymath, it's like having a polymath in your pocket.

196
00:21:08,700 --> 00:21:20,720
Now, just imagine a world in which you'd have any researcher, any student, have essentially a virtual lab under working for them.

197
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:38,260
Anybody could ask a research question, can have the system going through the literature, generate a hypothesis, validate them in the future, probably also validate them through actual lab experiments when you have enough machinery to actually do that, and we see good progress on that across the industry.

198
00:21:40,100 --> 00:21:41,348
Accelerate that kind of ...

199
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:49,740
Essentially, every researcher is going to have what today only the minority, only a small fraction of researchers could have, only very senior head of labs.

200
00:21:50,542 --> 00:22:03,460
This actually means that everybody could ask bigger questions, make faster progress, and since we're no lack of questions in science that we need to answer, I think it's going to accelerate scientific discovery quite a bit.

201
00:22:03,918 --> 00:22:05,860
Now, this is only one part of the equation.

202
00:22:06,670 --> 00:22:17,580
because if you think about a scientist, think about a biologist, or a chemist, or material design scientist, they can be extremely good in their domain.

203
00:22:18,030 --> 00:22:23,920
but today, in order to really build models that check hypothesis, that alone is a lot of work.

204
00:22:24,460 --> 00:22:31,960
In fact, it can take days or weeks or months, or sometimes you just need to hire data scientists to help you build the models.

205
00:22:32,718 --> 00:22:53,680
We have this system called Empirical Research Assistant, which essentially, again, using generative AI to help do this kind of, for any problem that is well-defined and for any input for that problem, it can help actually find, search and find and build the right models to help you solve them.

206
00:22:54,700 --> 00:23:02,040
And by the way, both co-scientist and empirical research assistant were both published in Nature just a couple of weeks ago.

207
00:23:02,990 --> 00:23:19,620
And by that time, we already had also quite a few papers in a host of domains and anything from cosmology to epidemiology to engineering and economics, that we're using empirical research assistance to actually help accelerate the research itself and solve problems that were previously open.

208
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:40,300
Again, if you think about this combination of taking a billion hypothesis, doing literature search and we made them actually available through something we called recently, through something we called Gemini for Science, and by the way, this is a collaboration across many organizations, many teams across Google, and make it possible for researchers.

209
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:52,040
Take the empirical research system that together with another system developed by DeepMind called AlphaEvolve, actually is feeding into something that we call computational discovery.

210
00:23:53,422 --> 00:24:00,180
And other areas such as literature insights, these are pieces in the scientific method in accelerating research.

211
00:24:00,180 --> 00:24:00,540
research.

212
00:24:01,166 --> 00:24:08,480
And there are only some of the pieces because one other aspect that we really should care about is how are we going to review scientific literature.

213
00:24:09,230 --> 00:24:16,920
So we're also looking into experimenting with paper assistant tools that can actually help you actually get feedback on your paper.

214
00:24:17,320 --> 00:24:20,620
And in the future, I envision also reviewers using AI.

215
00:24:21,102 --> 00:24:23,500
In fact, the scientific method is more important than ever.

216
00:24:24,078 --> 00:24:41,060
And using AI to actually help assist with scientific method is going to be paramount to help us actually accelerate scientific discovery in a way that is rigorous, in a way that we can actually build those scientific layers that we can actually enable to look further into the next research questions.

217
00:24:41,486 --> 00:24:48,980
So I think that we see this acceleration, which I think is one of the most exciting aspects of using AI to accelerate science itself.

218
00:24:49,300 --> 00:24:55,160
Right, so things that could have taken years, we might see in months, weeks, or days.

219
00:24:55,280 --> 00:25:06,920
And I think maybe the average person doesn't realize When you have a hypothesis that you want to solve or to check, all of the work that goes into researching whether this is the route you should go down, right?

220
00:25:07,120 --> 00:25:11,540
If you, the example you gave, is this, why did this bacteria become antibiotic resistant?

221
00:25:11,982 --> 00:25:15,080
Well, now you have to go research everything that's been written about that bacteria.

222
00:25:15,200 --> 00:25:19,340
Maybe somebody else has already proved or disproved that, why something becomes antibiotic resistant.

223
00:25:19,520 --> 00:25:23,960
That could take months just to understand, is this the hypothesis worth pursuing?

224
00:25:24,430 --> 00:25:27,980
And then you mentioned with empirical research assistant, Well, maybe you're a chemist.

225
00:25:28,238 --> 00:25:31,960
Now you might have to code a model to actually test that hypothesis.

226
00:25:32,140 --> 00:25:35,760
So maybe you bring in a computer scientist, or you yourself have to now start suddenly code.

227
00:25:36,046 --> 00:25:40,020
So now you have this virtual team around you that can start to do all of this.

228
00:25:40,302 --> 00:25:46,200
And I loved in one of your talks when you said, most people don't realize that Edison had about 100 people working with him, right?

229
00:25:46,340 --> 00:25:49,480
A lot of these top scientists, they have this factory around them of people.

230
00:25:49,870 --> 00:25:51,620
And so now this is essentially what's been created.

231
00:25:52,654 --> 00:25:58,660
But is there now, so if you think about all these tools, is there a skills gap emerging?

232
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,940
Because it's one thing to have this virtual lab, it's another thing to know how to use it.

233
00:26:02,286 --> 00:26:06,060
And on a call we had a couple days ago, you stated something that was really interesting to me.

234
00:26:06,140 --> 00:26:14,180
You said, we have a gap in how to accelerate training for scientists to see themselves as architects.

235
00:26:14,798 --> 00:26:16,600
What did you mean by the scientists as the architect?

236
00:26:17,102 --> 00:26:23,600
So first, yeah, so indeed, you know, Edison, who's probably the ultimate inventor, has an invention factory.

237
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:25,740
He was not doing it alone, he had his lab.

238
00:26:26,020 --> 00:26:32,860
When you think about our most esteemed, admired scientists today, they have a lab actually working with them.

239
00:26:33,380 --> 00:26:43,260
And now we're getting into a world where actually everyone is going to have their own virtual lab, which I think about it as AI as an amplifier of human ingenuity.

240
00:26:44,014 --> 00:26:47,540
Now the role of the researcher is going to become even more important than ever.

241
00:26:47,540 --> 00:27:04,760
I mean, there's sometimes this optics of, wait a minute, if a grad student or a postdoc of a junior scientist who spent a lot of their time actually doing literature search and building models, now they can get it for free, what remains to be done?

242
00:27:05,166 --> 00:27:05,980
Well, here's the point.

243
00:27:06,340 --> 00:27:13,620
The world is even more important than ever, because when you can actually have a virtual lab in your disposal, you can actually ask questions.

244
00:27:13,620 --> 00:27:18,800
Essentially, you can do the same thing that today only very senior researchers are doing.

245
00:27:19,540 --> 00:27:22,300
So I think about it as AI is an amplifier of human ingenuity.

246
00:27:23,182 --> 00:27:25,420
But that's not a prediction, that's a design goal.

247
00:27:25,806 --> 00:27:33,040
Because when you think about it, what does it take for a junior scientist to actually operate as if they have a virtual lab on their disposal?

248
00:27:33,200 --> 00:27:35,540
Today, it takes a year to grow into that.

249
00:27:36,206 --> 00:27:41,560
You do the more junior work and then you observe and you learn.

250
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:52,720
So, in a world where we can actually say, you know, you can actually start asking these questions early on, we need to actually fill that gap and, in a way, train grad students.

251
00:27:53,280 --> 00:27:55,460
And by the way, this is true for any domain, I believe.

252
00:27:55,860 --> 00:27:59,060
It's true also for engineering, computer science, biomedical.

253
00:27:59,340 --> 00:28:01,300
It's also for probably healthcare workers.

254
00:28:01,486 --> 00:28:01,740
Law, too.

255
00:28:01,840 --> 00:28:02,540
Yeah, with all these tools.

256
00:28:02,860 --> 00:28:16,800
Because AI is actually taking all these kind of, if you will, entry-level tasks, which allows everybody to actually step into the more advanced stage of asking the right questions, giving the right guidance, and having a judgment about the results.

257
00:28:17,262 --> 00:28:19,100
Because the results are never kind of binary.

258
00:28:19,340 --> 00:28:21,200
It's not about, here's the question, here's the answer.

259
00:28:21,580 --> 00:28:24,240
It's always, here are the possibilities, here are the trade-offs.

260
00:28:24,740 --> 00:28:25,480
Is this good?

261
00:28:26,222 --> 00:28:28,080
Do you compare these different parameters?

262
00:28:28,280 --> 00:28:31,000
So the judgment call is important, more important than ever.

263
00:28:31,502 --> 00:28:33,900
So there are a few things, a few observations.

264
00:28:33,900 --> 00:28:46,120
One, we need to see how to adapt our systems, our studies, our grad studies, et cetera, so as to train for using AI to actually build into that.

265
00:28:47,182 --> 00:29:03,520
I'm quite optimistic that we can actually use AI to help with that as well, because AI can provide, for example, on scientific method, AI can help provide feedback on papers early on, which is one of the time-consuming tasks for any advisor of grad students, for example, and so forth.

266
00:29:03,598 --> 00:29:05,500
So, this is one observation that we can have.

267
00:29:06,080 --> 00:29:18,420
Another one is this observation that having a judgment call both about what is the important question is usually going to become even more paramount, but also looking and judging if an answer is the right one.

268
00:29:18,766 --> 00:29:19,960
Sometimes it's an obvious one.

269
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:20,620
You have an optimization.

270
00:29:21,100 --> 00:29:23,580
You know, I'd like to solve for this number.

271
00:29:23,980 --> 00:29:24,900
You can get the check.

272
00:29:25,020 --> 00:29:26,200
You can check out the result.

273
00:29:26,360 --> 00:29:28,240
You can actually verify whether it's the best one.

274
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:34,740
Sometimes it's a judgment call because you want a solution that applies to a system that is complex and has trade-offs.

275
00:29:35,406 --> 00:29:37,380
Yeah, I'd actually like to borrow a quote from.

276
00:29:38,414 --> 00:29:39,580
I love jazz.

277
00:29:39,620 --> 00:29:44,280
and one of my favorite quotes is Duke Ellington saying about music: "If.

278
00:29:44,280 --> 00:29:45,000
it sounds good.

279
00:29:45,454 --> 00:29:58,220
it is good ,", which is pretty profound, because essentially the judgment of what is good is actually the thing that we're going to rely more and more on humans, actually, to do.

280
00:29:58,830 --> 00:30:05,800
Of course, AI systems are going to learn and then apply it, and then we're going to ask given bigger questions and still need to have this judgment.

281
00:30:06,350 --> 00:30:11,740
And that's why I think science education, some people think, oh, it's gonna get easy, now we have all these technologies.

282
00:30:12,142 --> 00:30:25,860
I think it's actually gonna be harder because what you're describing is a world where the skills that somebody would have had 10, 15 years to build, we're gonna start to expect that of junior scientists soon because they can ask questions that somebody had 40 years to figure out.

283
00:30:26,200 --> 00:30:28,940
Now you have the, it's possible for you to answer those questions.

284
00:30:29,040 --> 00:30:31,340
You need to build the judgment skills to be able to do that.

285
00:30:31,342 --> 00:30:34,400
So, I think that's kind of where all of society is going in education.

286
00:30:34,920 --> 00:30:35,280
Certainly.

287
00:30:35,460 --> 00:30:37,560
I think this is, again, this optical illusion.

288
00:30:38,220 --> 00:30:39,360
We've seen it before, right?

289
00:30:39,400 --> 00:30:46,500
I mean, when Google and Wikipedia became kind of a commodity, if you will, available to everybody to use, then there were concerns.

290
00:30:46,640 --> 00:30:55,380
So, are our kids going to be stupid or lazy because we used to give them homework to actually go to the library and collect facts, and now they can actually do that in a few minutes?

291
00:30:55,950 --> 00:30:57,160
Well, we adopted, right?

292
00:30:57,180 --> 00:30:59,980
As a society, we actually have a higher expectation.

293
00:30:59,980 --> 00:31:01,560
This is now assumed to be given.

294
00:31:02,140 --> 00:31:06,880
Now, of course, now we're elevating what's expected and what is given.

295
00:31:07,560 --> 00:31:20,340
When you think about even mathematics, we see now so many announcements of researchers or sometimes kids actually saying, hey, we use AI to actually solve that Erdos problem or that Erdos problem.

296
00:31:21,220 --> 00:31:28,460
This is a bank of mathematical problems actually that was put by Paul Erdos, a legendary mathematician.

297
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:31,660
and which were open for a long time.

298
00:31:32,440 --> 00:31:38,040
So there's a certain illusion for a moment that, hey, actually all these research questions are going to be solved.

299
00:31:38,542 --> 00:31:41,780
Well, not quite, because of course, some of them are going to be solved.

300
00:31:41,920 --> 00:31:43,400
Many of them are actually going to accelerate.

301
00:31:44,014 --> 00:31:45,580
Now we can ask bigger questions.

302
00:31:46,190 --> 00:31:50,140
So I think that there's never a lack here of what we need to solve.

303
00:31:50,600 --> 00:31:52,080
Similarly, think about healthcare.

304
00:31:52,974 --> 00:32:00,780
We did some progress, but there's so much work to be done in order to really, you know, how do we solve healthcare?

305
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:05,280
How do we make sure that nobody's surprised by a medical condition?

306
00:32:05,540 --> 00:32:07,240
How do we actually find early detection?

307
00:32:07,400 --> 00:32:09,572
How do we find treatments for cancer?

308
00:32:09,920 --> 00:32:12,380
How do we actually solve public health?

309
00:32:12,814 --> 00:32:18,720
These are all questions that were so early on, so we should actually embrace and use AI to actually help solve them.

310
00:32:18,900 --> 00:32:19,020
Right.

311
00:32:19,060 --> 00:32:21,740
We need more people actually in the field now powered by these technologies.

312
00:32:22,670 --> 00:32:25,600
is there going to be a bottleneck with validation?

313
00:32:25,920 --> 00:32:31,380
Because you mentioned now AI can, it's not limited to a certain academic discipline the way we are, right?

314
00:32:31,440 --> 00:32:36,500
Every few decades we get a polymath that can go across disciplines, but that's usually rare.

315
00:32:36,620 --> 00:32:45,780
You study chemistry, you study physics, you study biology, but it's possible that AI could spot a pattern that exists across disciplines but doesn't make sense within a discipline.

316
00:32:46,350 --> 00:32:53,140
Who is going to be equipped to make that call that that does make sense if we've been wired to go discipline by discipline.

317
00:32:53,400 --> 00:33:04,260
So let me actually start with the notion of disciplines and interdisciplinary thing, because to me, actually, this is one of the biggest opportunities that we see now with AI, actually, as accelerating scientific discovery.

318
00:33:04,942 --> 00:33:12,540
The way in which scientific world is built is indeed typically pushing for particular disciplines.

319
00:33:13,262 --> 00:33:19,880
And what I found out again and again is that when you bring together multiple disciplines, that's a lot of the magic happens.

320
00:33:20,366 --> 00:33:22,880
You can actually borrow, see, and connect knowledge.

321
00:33:23,598 --> 00:33:27,040
Typically, you don't have many people who actually are cross-disciplines.

322
00:33:27,720 --> 00:33:31,280
Those who are actually in cross-disciplines, a lot of the good stuff happens.

323
00:33:31,420 --> 00:33:32,900
Now with AI, we can actually do that.

324
00:33:33,280 --> 00:33:39,700
I'm really optimistic that this is going to accelerate and enable us actually to bring together multiple areas.

325
00:33:40,700 --> 00:33:50,040
By the way, also, the question about validation is a critical one because in the world that And AI can generate hypotheses, and so many of them.

326
00:33:50,606 --> 00:33:57,240
The notion of checking them, validating them, making the right judgment about them becomes increasingly more important.

327
00:33:58,126 --> 00:34:00,880
So obviously, we need to use AI to help with that as well.

328
00:34:01,720 --> 00:34:15,020
And by the way, in a world where we can actually make more progress across verticals, across domains, I think that also have education that is broader is going to become increasingly more important.

329
00:34:16,045 --> 00:34:21,739
So I think this is one positive direction that I think we're going to see in the future.

330
00:34:22,400 --> 00:34:24,480
And obviously encouraging also people.

331
00:34:24,580 --> 00:34:29,159
So again, it's always a trade-off between going deep in a particular area versus going broad.

332
00:34:30,094 --> 00:34:41,840
Now, in many cases, going deep was required because in order to really solve your problem all the way, you needed to do everything from the lab work or to building models or to solving all the theorems and everything.

333
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:54,400
But in the world that you already have a virtual lab in your disposal, then we're going to do some adaptation of what is the level that you need to train and how to actually find the right balance of how we can actually educate ourselves.

334
00:34:54,800 --> 00:34:56,520
And by the way, this is true not only for science.

335
00:34:56,699 --> 00:34:58,860
I believe this is going to be true also in technology.

336
00:34:59,100 --> 00:35:05,920
We used to have software engineers and product managers and user interface designers and et cetera.

337
00:35:05,920 --> 00:35:21,360
And suddenly, I envision, I see that we're now getting into a world that actually with AI, you can have, everybody actually have a team of virtual engineers doing some work for them, and a team of, et cetera.

338
00:35:21,480 --> 00:35:23,660
So you can actually have many of these functions, actually.

339
00:35:24,206 --> 00:35:29,600
So the ability to actually have the skills across disciplines is going to become extremely more important.

340
00:35:30,060 --> 00:35:30,240
Right.

341
00:35:30,300 --> 00:35:41,580
I like to say the idea of a job title, it's going to be harder for that to hold because now we're going to be, it's more about the skills underneath the things that we're doing and the specific taxonomy that had worked in the industrial era.

342
00:35:41,742 --> 00:35:43,760
But now we're going to be able to move much more fluidly.

343
00:35:43,840 --> 00:35:48,900
So whether it's you're in product and now you're kind of also doing engineering or even cross-disciplinary in science.

344
00:35:49,294 --> 00:35:56,220
For someone who is not a scientist, what will they notice about their life when science gets accelerated by AI?

345
00:35:56,782 --> 00:35:57,800
Why should they care?

346
00:35:57,880 --> 00:35:58,620
What does it mean for them?

347
00:35:59,054 --> 00:36:16,100
So, first, one thing that I think we're having with these AI systems, agentic systems that are capable to actually take the tasks that today you actually need experts, is that it's going to open up opportunities for many more people to actually take a role, an important role in many disciplines.

348
00:36:17,294 --> 00:36:23,700
So, for example, you already have people who never actually had experience coding develop applications.

349
00:36:24,334 --> 00:36:32,640
And I actually heard somebody working in a startup and he was telling me, hey, I'm actually working with our customers and they're asking me for a feature.

350
00:36:33,540 --> 00:36:37,680
And in many cases, I actually don't need to actually ask our engineers to do anything here.

351
00:36:37,902 --> 00:36:39,820
I can actually do the prototype on my own.

352
00:36:40,240 --> 00:36:41,020
Think about it.

353
00:36:41,080 --> 00:36:42,060
This is pretty powerful.

354
00:36:42,960 --> 00:36:50,060
Same thing if you are a scientist and you need some help on a different discipline, you don't need necessarily to go and spend time and find the right connection.

355
00:36:50,702 --> 00:36:52,580
You can actually use AI to help you with that.

356
00:36:53,454 --> 00:36:59,160
So, this notion of connecting disciplines is going to be increasingly powerful.

357
00:36:59,440 --> 00:37:13,764
By the way, as an interesting point, when I think back, even when I just started at Google almost 20 years ago now, we always wanted to make sure that product managers have education in computer science.

358
00:37:14,574 --> 00:37:18,540
So, even if their job is not to write code, it's to understand how to write code.

359
00:37:19,086 --> 00:37:22,840
We always expect our researchers to be able to write code.

360
00:37:23,630 --> 00:37:31,680
We always expected our engineers to be able to solve research questions, so this notion of understanding the different disciplines was always there.

361
00:37:32,206 --> 00:37:36,420
Now I think that the opportunity is even greater for anybody to actually open up.

362
00:37:36,900 --> 00:37:40,200
Now many more people can actually then develop applications and write code.

363
00:37:40,280 --> 00:37:57,124
Many more people can actually become experts in a particular domain, and with AI they can actually make a difference in scientific research by actually applying the right research skills and in a way that was unthinkable in the past.

364
00:37:57,380 --> 00:37:57,620
Right.

365
00:37:57,720 --> 00:37:58,980
So we're in the era of vibe coding.

366
00:37:59,180 --> 00:38:03,100
Soon we go to vibe drug discovery, where just the average person is coming up with molecules.

367
00:38:03,540 --> 00:38:09,020
With flood prediction, is it finally understanding the signals of the planet that it's always been giving off?

368
00:38:10,190 --> 00:38:13,060
Let me start, actually, with a question of why did we look into flood prediction?

369
00:38:14,060 --> 00:38:19,000
I think I mentioned earlier that we always need to start with the why.

370
00:38:19,470 --> 00:38:21,260
What is the impact of what we're going to do?

371
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:31,880
In this context, actually, I've been overseeing what we call crisis resilience or crisis response for now a decade or so.

372
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:34,460
People are coming to Google during crisis.

373
00:38:34,926 --> 00:38:44,676
So we actually look into how can we actually give them with actionable information for any crisis that they have and in fact launched it serves something that we call SOS alerts.

374
00:38:45,518 --> 00:38:51,000
But one thing I discovered is that we are as helpful, of course, that as the information that we can actually share.

375
00:38:51,000 --> 00:39:03,860
and as it turns out, the most devastating natural disasters, floods, which are responsible for thousands of casualties every year, were not helpful, because the only way to be helpful is actually to give —the best.

376
00:39:03,860 --> 00:39:08,040
way to be helpful is to to give predictions about floods coming so that people can take action.

377
00:39:08,860 --> 00:39:19,760
Now, a decade ago when I asked people about the experts, flood experts, what can we do in order to provide prediction, the general consensus was that this is too difficult.

378
00:39:20,590 --> 00:39:26,000
Because in order to have a valuable prediction, you need to have high confidence prediction, too many variables and so forth.

379
00:39:26,862 --> 00:39:34,880
So what we really looked into is, well, everybody says it's too difficult, but it's an important problem to tackle.

380
00:39:34,880 --> 00:39:37,120
Let's see if we can make progress about it.

381
00:39:37,900 --> 00:39:41,060
We started actually with some research.

382
00:39:43,300 --> 00:39:55,260
Once we had enough progress, we actually ran a pilot in India and showed that we can actually do some reasonable prediction there under very tight constraints, but enough to make me optimistic.

383
00:39:55,540 --> 00:40:03,780
In fact, we even published a paper back in almost eight years ago with a hypothesis that in the future we're going to be able to scale with machine learning in the cloud.

384
00:40:04,462 --> 00:40:14,520
I had no idea how we're going to do that, but there was enough indications that we can actually take all these variables and try to use machine learning in order to do that.

385
00:40:15,246 --> 00:40:37,640
It did require many cycles of going from research to trying it out in the field, to identifying what we can do to work with partnerships, with academics, with governments to actually who collect the data that we could actually build machine learning models on that, and eventually actually help drive the science for that.

386
00:40:37,860 --> 00:40:42,000
We published a paper in Nature with what we call global hydrologic model.

387
00:40:43,580 --> 00:40:56,480
And really what we showed is that we can actually build models that are learning from flood events in places that we have enough data and then apply it also to places that we don't have as much data.

388
00:40:56,480 --> 00:41:15,100
And what started as an impossible problem, quote-unquote, we actually now not only showed that we can drive the science, but also the same team, we've built a system that now provides up to seven days prediction in 150 countries, covering two billion people.

389
00:41:15,598 --> 00:41:22,880
So it's already life-saving, because it's out there, it's available for responders, for governments, through what we call Flathub.

390
00:41:23,480 --> 00:41:32,100
So, this is an example for this iterating, starting with a problem, taking a research step, bringing it to reality, asking the next question.

391
00:41:32,500 --> 00:41:35,220
I like to call this the magic cycle of research.

392
00:41:35,918 --> 00:41:44,840
So, in a way, one reason why research is more exciting than ever is because this magic cycle is accelerating more than ever before.

393
00:41:45,358 --> 00:41:47,840
And it's even further accelerating with the use of AI.

394
00:41:48,398 --> 00:41:51,140
We can actually use AI to actually drive the research faster.

395
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:55,524
we can then use AI to help bring the research to reality.

396
00:41:56,200 --> 00:41:57,820
And that has tremendous impact.

397
00:41:57,870 --> 00:41:59,720
In this case, it's actually life-saving.

398
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:01,720
That's extraordinary, right?

399
00:42:02,222 --> 00:42:07,420
Imagine getting a notification seven days in advance of a flood, that there's going to be a flood.

400
00:42:07,886 --> 00:42:09,300
That's truly life-changing.

401
00:42:09,640 --> 00:42:21,740
And, you know, just a few months ago, we had a government of Nigeria and organizations called GiveDirectly actually use an API to our flood hub to actually send money to villagers so they can evacuate ahead of time.

402
00:42:22,670 --> 00:42:31,540
But that's actually not the end of the story, because in any given moment, you're getting into a certain level of, here's what we can do, now here are the problems that we haven't solved as yet.

403
00:42:32,110 --> 00:42:41,040
For example, in the context of floods, we faced another wall, which is, all these models are true for what we call river and floods.

404
00:42:41,220 --> 00:42:43,000
These are rivers that actually go overboard.

405
00:42:43,600 --> 00:42:46,720
Now, some of the devastating floods are actually flash floods.

406
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:54,460
These are floods that come, surprisingly, They come in a way that typically we don't have enough data to actually build models for them.

407
00:42:55,982 --> 00:42:59,560
Until about last year, actually, this was kind of an unsolved problem.

408
00:43:00,560 --> 00:43:06,840
Here comes actually the next innovation, which was an observation that we could actually use.

409
00:43:07,580 --> 00:43:13,604
All these flood events that we care about, at least in urban areas, typically are reported in news.

410
00:43:15,440 --> 00:43:29,060
The idea was to actually use generative AI, to use GemIIni in order to read through news of 20 years, identify events of floods reported in the news, which also described quite often when they happened.

411
00:43:29,200 --> 00:43:36,324
This is all public information in many languages, and essentially build a database of events.

412
00:43:36,942 --> 00:43:38,780
We call this technique ground source.

413
00:43:39,118 --> 00:43:44,800
Essentially, build a ground truth data so that we can build AI models on them.

414
00:43:45,380 --> 00:43:56,500
So we used, actually, generative AI to build and to identify 2.6 million flash floods events, which then we could actually build machine learning models, AI models based on them.

415
00:43:57,040 --> 00:44:02,860
And we made this prediction for urban flash floods available as well on Flathub.

416
00:44:02,894 --> 00:44:17,260
So, a problem that was just practically unsolved just a year ago is now with the combination of generative AI and the observations that you can actually use this data and have this connection to proxies, we can actually then solve problems.

417
00:44:17,860 --> 00:44:37,476
Now, the paradigm itself is not something, you know, I developed Google Trends with my team many years ago, and one paradigm that we've seen very useful is how to use Google trends, which essentially people termed sometimes the database of intentions, to identify various phenomenas.

418
00:44:38,080 --> 00:44:49,820
So to identify phenomenas of outbreaks and phenomenas of political events and otherwise just based on trends of aggregations.

419
00:44:50,318 --> 00:44:52,920
Now with the ground source, we can take it to the next level.

420
00:44:53,518 --> 00:44:56,420
And we actually make it available for others to use as well.

421
00:44:56,846 --> 00:45:10,160
And with Urban Flash Flood, this is an example of how to bring generative AI along with public information and make it possible to actually solve problems of societal challenge that could actually have life-saving impact.

422
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:13,440
And what would be your North Star for crisis resilience?

423
00:45:13,720 --> 00:45:14,940
Where are you going?

424
00:45:15,438 --> 00:45:16,140
Yeah, it's a great question.

425
00:45:16,260 --> 00:45:24,920
So obviously, flood prediction is one such problem, but there's a lot of research work done on improving, for example, storm prediction.

426
00:45:24,920 --> 00:45:39,000
and our colleagues in Google DeepMind is doing an amazing job on actually driving research on that and working in collaboration with the Google research to actually bring in what we call WeatherNext as helping predictions of weather, extreme weather, some sort.

427
00:45:39,406 --> 00:45:41,020
We look into wildfire detection.

428
00:45:41,860 --> 00:45:49,100
And here, again, we're looking into how to use satellite imagery in order to help predict wildfires and even are engaged.

429
00:45:49,340 --> 00:46:01,860
we set up this, you know, engagement with other partners on what we call FARSAT, essentially an initiative to bring to orbit 50 satellites, 50.

430
00:46:01,902 --> 00:46:06,660
some satellites that eventually, once they're in orbit, are going to enable to have flood prediction.

431
00:46:06,660 --> 00:46:14,580
every point on Earth, every 20 minutes, identifying wildfire at the size of this room, and so forth.

432
00:46:14,720 --> 00:46:21,260
Now, when I think about all these crisis resilience tasks, when I think about what is our objective, where are we heading?

433
00:46:21,260 --> 00:46:23,120
The North Star is very simple.

434
00:46:23,740 --> 00:46:27,560
Nobody should ever be surprised by a natural disaster coming their way.

435
00:46:28,270 --> 00:46:30,280
So, that's actually what we're trying to solve for.

436
00:46:30,638 --> 00:46:34,040
How can we use prediction so that people can be prepared?

437
00:46:34,542 --> 00:46:41,180
So that both to keep themselves safe, as well as their property, as well as take actions.

438
00:46:41,838 --> 00:46:44,740
So, I think that's where we're trying to be heading.

439
00:46:44,740 --> 00:47:00,620
Now, these all technologies are, obviously, in order to have the right kind of questions here, you need to be able to ask questions about the world, about floods, about weather, about storms, about heat, and so forth.

440
00:47:01,582 --> 00:47:08,836
At some point, we also observed that, you know what, in fact, what we really want is to have planetary intelligence.

441
00:47:08,880 --> 00:47:17,020
So, what if we take all these geospatial models and we're bringing, you know, satellite imagery, we build additional models of satellites.

442
00:47:17,486 --> 00:47:18,320
we bring data.

443
00:47:18,960 --> 00:47:31,060
we bring a model that we developed called population dynamics, which essentially is a foundation model about people's movements that was actually quite important for, you know, during COVID analysis and so forth.

444
00:47:31,886 --> 00:47:44,320
What if we bring them all together into a family of model that we call Google Earth AI, and we actually have an agentic layer, an AI agentic layer on top of that, so you can actually ask any question about the planet.

445
00:47:45,326 --> 00:47:52,720
For example, in the context of crisis resilience, you can ask not only a question, where is the flood or storm going to hit, but what are the most vulnerable communities?

446
00:47:53,294 --> 00:47:55,740
What are the actions that you need in order to evacuate them?

447
00:47:56,046 --> 00:48:00,880
What's the infrastructure that is going to be affected, and what actions do you need to do up front?

448
00:48:01,742 --> 00:48:02,756
Think about public health.

449
00:48:03,342 --> 00:48:17,680
In fact, there was recently a paper published by some partners that we have in Mount Sinai, Boston Children's Hospital and Harvard about how to analyze measles vaccination in the zip code level using EarthAI.

450
00:48:18,382 --> 00:48:24,540
And if you think about public health or epidemiology, much of it is really about connecting

451
00:48:29,100 --> 00:48:43,680
you to earth, to places, to various things that are to economic situations in those places, to other factors, such as who got vaccine and who did not get vaccine in terms of in aggregate level.

452
00:48:44,942 --> 00:48:55,600
So there are already a bunch of works done by many partners on public health, including partners such as Cooper Smith, about WHO and others.

453
00:48:56,046 --> 00:49:05,160
Obviously, looking into how can we help with the cholera outbreak or now the Ebola outbreak with EarthAI.

454
00:49:05,870 --> 00:49:07,660
So, public health is another big area.

455
00:49:07,820 --> 00:49:09,700
Crisis resilience is another big area.

456
00:49:10,400 --> 00:49:18,760
Even for businesses, if you're in the business of – many businesses actually care about what's happening from the planetary point of view.

457
00:49:19,200 --> 00:49:29,480
So, for example, it can be even the marketing business, WPP is a partner using EarthAI to ask business questions, or if you're a logistic company or a storage company, you want to ask questions about the planet.

458
00:49:30,030 --> 00:49:32,240
It can be about understanding phenomena.

459
00:49:32,520 --> 00:49:39,364
It can be about, again, public health, crisis resilience, or even just understanding for scientific or education purposes.

460
00:49:39,918 --> 00:49:50,800
So I think this notion of using AI as a platform to have planetary intelligence is a fascinating way for us to elevate everything that we do.

461
00:49:51,182 --> 00:49:59,040
It's no longer solving only one problem at a time, but bringing them together and using AI to actually connect it all, make it seamless.

462
00:49:59,440 --> 00:50:18,660
And this seems, if we were to pull a thread from the ambient intelligence conversation we had earlier, it seems like you have companies trying to build this intelligence layer for their organization, and then Google is building an intelligence layer for the planet, where you can have, essentially, a conversation with the planet and with the world, right?

463
00:50:18,660 --> 00:50:32,120
in some of the examples I was reading in the papers, I mean, you could be a conservationist and you could ask Earth AI, you know, where we have a city under pressure to build a road, is forest A or is forest B more critical to a certain species?

464
00:50:32,686 --> 00:50:45,880
Or you could be a health expert that asks, you know, where is this cholera outbreak going to go next without even needing to know or understand all of the intelligence and all of the different models, epidemiology, geospatial communication that's happening behind it.

465
00:50:46,080 --> 00:50:49,520
So isn't this kind of that on-ramp to that ambient intelligence layer?

466
00:50:50,180 --> 00:51:02,000
In a way it is, because again, this is part of the beauty of building a platform is that you build a new base for everybody to actually work on and then they can focus on the next level.

467
00:51:02,520 --> 00:51:05,660
Let me give you another example from entirely different domain, which is healthcare.

468
00:51:06,670 --> 00:51:08,660
Again, in healthcare, you build models.

469
00:51:08,880 --> 00:51:12,200
You want to actually understand both language models.

470
00:51:13,102 --> 00:51:21,000
So for example, a few years ago, we actually looked into the question, Can we have the language model understand medical information in a way?

471
00:51:21,160 --> 00:51:27,700
And we took as a benchmark the question of a language model taking a US-style medical exam.

472
00:51:28,206 --> 00:51:36,980
In fact, we showed for the first time that we could actually adapt a language model for the first time to pass the US-style medical exam in a passing score.

473
00:51:37,678 --> 00:51:47,500
By the time this was published in Nature, we already had a model that could pass it in an expert level, 85% versus 65, and 91% with multimodality.

474
00:51:48,520 --> 00:51:54,540
Importantly, this was already being used by partners to test out various applications.

475
00:51:56,340 --> 00:51:58,720
Again, this is what I call the magic cycle of research.

476
00:51:58,860 --> 00:52:03,080
It's about asking the research questions about applying it, asking the next research question.

477
00:52:04,686 --> 00:52:15,100
After doing that, also, we ask ourselves, how can we actually take those capabilities is that we add to models and make them more widely available for others to build on.

478
00:52:15,780 --> 00:52:22,720
So to that extent, we also have this platform that we call HiDef, which essentially are open models that we can have others to build on.

479
00:52:23,520 --> 00:52:27,020
And we actually developed something we called MedJama.

480
00:52:27,598 --> 00:52:30,700
So Jama is our open source model for language model.

481
00:52:30,800 --> 00:52:32,760
This is the open source version for Gemini.

482
00:52:33,326 --> 00:52:42,940
MedJerma is taking Jerma and building on that are some of our best possible medical models that we can actually build on that and on all sorts.

483
00:52:43,726 --> 00:52:46,340
And we made it available just a little over a year ago.

484
00:52:46,894 --> 00:52:48,980
We already have more than 5 million downloads.

485
00:52:50,000 --> 00:53:08,800
And as part of those downloads, we also hear about thousands of applications about how to, and the baseline for this application is that you already have a model that understands medical information better, and you can also use it for various applications, such as, for example, have a speech recognition of medical terms.

486
00:53:08,960 --> 00:53:10,920
This is one of the models that are there, for example.

487
00:53:11,760 --> 00:53:26,700
And for example, I heard from a startup that is looking into how to build foundational models for eye diseases, that they're taking MedGemma as a baseline, saving them perhaps a few years of a lot of investments, and already building what they need in order to solve their own problem.

488
00:53:27,374 --> 00:53:40,500
And more recently, I heard actually about a use case of a healthcare worker in Uganda in a village that encountered a stressful information of a woman who was about to give birth.

489
00:53:41,200 --> 00:53:50,020
And she didn't have any internet connection, but she had an app that was using Medjema to actually be able to get some advice.

490
00:53:50,400 --> 00:53:50,600
Right.

491
00:53:50,620 --> 00:53:52,760
And she managed to get advice on the condition.

492
00:53:52,760 --> 00:53:58,680
This was a third-party app built on Magemma that might have saved both the mother and the baby.

493
00:53:59,420 --> 00:54:03,540
So, again, this is one case out of many that probably we don't know of.

494
00:54:04,110 --> 00:54:05,680
That's the power of having a platform.

495
00:54:05,820 --> 00:54:10,340
That's the power of actually making models open, available for others to use.

496
00:54:10,800 --> 00:54:11,590
By the way,

497
00:54:11,590 --> 00:54:18,740
recently we made, just a few days ago, we made our flood forecasting models open-sourced so that others can build on that as well.

498
00:54:19,022 --> 00:54:19,260
Right.

499
00:54:19,500 --> 00:54:21,880
Artificial intelligence, it's not the ceiling, right?

500
00:54:21,880 --> 00:54:26,520
it becomes the new floor from which scientists and society springboard off of.

501
00:54:26,860 --> 00:54:30,700
So everybody rises up on top of AI.

502
00:54:30,980 --> 00:54:31,160
Right.

503
00:54:31,438 --> 00:54:32,900
And of course, this is only one direction.

504
00:54:33,486 --> 00:54:51,800
If we talk about healthcare, which is one of the areas where AI obviously is going to have a huge impact on society and on people, you know, building on our capabilities on understanding language, we also start asking ourselves, how can we actually integrate it into the workflow in various levels.

505
00:54:52,560 --> 00:55:02,200
You know, there are the traditional levels, traditional AI-wise, for example, we had some research on diabetic retinopathy, which is a condition, actually, that can save from blindness.

506
00:55:02,200 --> 00:55:04,620
This is now a decade-old research.

507
00:55:05,320 --> 00:55:20,080
But we brought it to reality over the years with our partners in India and Thailand, and they now had over a million screenings which can prevent blindness from thousands of people, many thousands of people.

508
00:55:20,526 --> 00:55:45,260
Or we recently published research with the NHS about how to use machine learning that helps with mammography, to help actually see how we can actually get into a workflow, and showing that the research —there were two papers in Nature, actually published showing that AI can help reduce by 25% %% the misses and bring back 40% %% time to the radiologists.

509
00:55:45,260 --> 00:55:49,700
So, these are steps in the research to see how can we actually get into reality.

510
00:55:50,720 --> 00:56:03,900
Another direction is to look into, well, if you think about the experience of medical diagnostics, a lot of it is really kind of the medical, the conversational experience with a doctor, with a practitioner.

511
00:56:04,878 --> 00:56:17,880
So the question we looked into, how can we use, given that we now have language models that now can provide trustworthy information about medical conditions, can we actually use it as part of the diagnostics to help drive the conversation.

512
00:56:18,606 --> 00:56:21,100
Testing the viability of this approach.

513
00:56:21,560 --> 00:56:34,880
And again, in a sequence of papers published in Nature and other venues showing that actually they can provide on various parameters, including, by the way, helpfulness and even empathy, be assistive.

514
00:56:35,580 --> 00:56:45,760
And just recently we announced collaboration with Included Health to test it in the field, actually, to see can it actually be useful and helpful to practitioners.

515
00:56:46,254 --> 00:57:02,200
Taking a very measured approach of testing things rigorously, publishing it so that we can actually see that we measure things correctly, then trying it in a good possible way, and so forth, and building on that.

516
00:57:02,360 --> 00:57:13,760
So again, just recently, when we published what we call the Google Health app, essentially providing people with information about their health based on sensory information from their watches.

517
00:57:15,438 --> 00:57:33,220
This is based, much of it is based on a sequence of researchers on using large language model in a personalized setting to see how can we actually take this information, convert it into insights that are helpful to people in a way that we rigorously test by research and bring it to reality.

518
00:57:33,678 --> 00:57:43,560
All of these are manifestation of this magic cycle of research, of us in the question, having the research, applying it to reality, take the next question and iterate on that.

519
00:57:44,494 --> 00:57:54,020
So if we were to look forward then, what do you think are the scientific questions that are gonna define the next decade for Google, but also for the world?

520
00:57:57,038 --> 00:58:08,510
So I think that when I think about scientific questions, and again, we're really early on in unlocking all the knowledge that we need in practically any domain I can think about.

521
00:58:08,510 --> 00:58:15,730
You know, we're, as much as we're excited about research breakthroughs, when you think about where are we with understanding how the brain works?

522
00:58:16,494 --> 00:58:17,850
Where are we with drug design?

523
00:58:18,574 --> 00:58:20,170
Where are we with material design?

524
00:58:21,070 --> 00:58:24,570
Where are we with really understanding the planet in a way that is predictable?

525
00:58:25,806 --> 00:58:27,250
Why should we be surprised?

526
00:58:27,410 --> 00:58:31,390
Why don't we actually know exactly what's going to happen and just plan for that?

527
00:58:31,430 --> 00:58:33,310
And this is going to have impact on our life.

528
00:58:33,998 --> 00:58:35,044
About food security.

529
00:58:36,750 --> 00:58:40,870
So, in each of these domains, there's a lot of work to be done, and or energy,

530
00:58:40,870 --> 00:58:41,170
right?

531
00:58:41,390 --> 00:58:45,110
I mean, we're talking a lot about energy and how to address that.

532
00:58:45,550 --> 00:58:55,350
But you know, with, I'm pretty sure that with scientific breakthroughs, we're going to see also breakthroughs in energy, in new sources, efficiency, and so forth.

533
00:58:56,238 --> 00:59:05,070
So these are all, when you think about all the big problems that we have in our life, on our daily life, on our interaction, how to...

534
00:59:05,326 --> 00:59:06,970
We touched earlier about education.

535
00:59:07,170 --> 00:59:10,150
To me, education is perhaps the single most important thing for any society.

536
00:59:10,290 --> 00:59:10,890
That's the future.

537
00:59:11,758 --> 00:59:29,050
If we are managing to let every person build to their talent and be able then to harness the next, the future AI technologies and technologies in general to actually solve other problems, that's a huge potential.

538
00:59:29,670 --> 00:59:32,750
That's actually where we have a huge headroom to do.

539
00:59:33,370 --> 00:59:37,630
And by the way, the role of a teacher is going to be more important than ever.

540
00:59:38,158 --> 00:59:45,490
We all have our inspirations, I'm sure, from teachers that actually help define what we are and what we chose in life.

541
00:59:46,730 --> 01:00:00,330
And hopefully this is going to enable even more teachers to inspire students and build on AI to actually accelerate what we can actually learn and make it even more fun, right?

542
01:00:01,870 --> 01:00:05,230
So these are all big opportunities, I think, that we have across the board.

543
01:00:05,910 --> 01:00:11,950
I think that one area that I'm really keen on is to see how we're actually going to help with creativity.

544
01:00:13,102 --> 01:00:16,670
And again, this is going to morph because AI is kind of becoming a tool.

545
01:00:17,570 --> 01:00:20,970
So many more are now going to be able to express themselves.

546
01:00:21,102 --> 01:00:23,750
It's going to make some changes in various disciplines.

547
01:00:25,570 --> 01:00:32,050
I think that creativity, humanity is actually the most important aspect of our lives.

548
01:00:32,142 --> 01:00:36,772
So obviously important to see where we're going to build on technology in order to amplify that.

549
01:00:37,390 --> 01:00:40,770
And it's something that I'm very keen to see how we're actually building on that.

550
01:00:41,358 --> 01:00:45,450
And where does quantum fall into all of this and when we think about the next decade?

551
01:00:45,934 --> 01:00:54,170
Because I feel like when you look at the timeline of quantum, so the last few decades have been about proving that the physics can work in a way that can scale.

552
01:00:54,730 --> 01:00:59,430
And I'll attempt to explain quantum for somebody who doesn't know about the world of quantum, and please correct the science.

553
01:01:00,010 --> 01:01:06,770
But qubits are the basic units of a quantum computer, and they are frustratingly fragile, right?

554
01:01:06,870 --> 01:01:17,030
But to build a quantum computer that can solve the great problems that we want them to solve from modeling a molecule or a complex system, you need to be able to add more qubits to the system.

555
01:01:17,470 --> 01:01:23,470
And historically, that has been very challenging, which is why Willow was such a big milestone, right?

556
01:01:23,850 --> 01:01:29,530
And you've been able to show you can build a larger quantum computer and it can actually become more reliable over time.

557
01:01:30,510 --> 01:01:37,710
So what is left to do in quantum to actually realize the true vision of quantum computing?

558
01:01:38,382 --> 01:01:45,230
And is the remaining phase harder, easier, or just a different kind of challenge than what's been done before?

559
01:01:46,254 --> 01:01:53,770
In quantum, of course, the notion of error correction has always been considered one of the barriers that we need to break.

560
01:01:54,790 --> 01:02:00,030
And we did good progress on that with the Willow chip just last year.

561
01:02:00,950 --> 01:02:06,770
So quantum is one of those magic cycles that are taking longer, if you will.

562
01:02:06,770 --> 01:02:16,484
In fact, some of the theoretical work, actually, that are the basis of what we're building in our quantum AI lab are going back to the 80s.

563
01:02:16,730 --> 01:02:26,290
The work of Michel de Voreil and John Martinez and John Clark, who were actually recognized with the Nobel Prize in physics just this year.

564
01:02:28,190 --> 01:02:33,630
And Michel, by the way, is leading, is chief scientist of our AI hardware team.

565
01:02:33,630 --> 01:02:51,570
Now, the premise of quantum computers is that we already know that because it's a different paradigm, because of the nature of qubits versus a classical computer, there are certain problems where a quantum computer can actually solve things much more faster than a classical computer.

566
01:02:52,110 --> 01:03:23,770
So, we had this example of a problem that was 10 subtillion years faster than a classical computer, 10 with 25 zeros, and more recently actually showed it on what we call a verified advantage, the echo algorithm, a problem that could actually be verified in other means and showing that with a quantum computer, we could solve it 13,000 times faster than a quantum, than a classical computer.

567
01:03:24,334 --> 01:03:39,910
There's obviously the implication of quantum computing on There are numbers here in particular cryptography, Shor's algorithm from the 90s that showed that quantum computers can solve a problem that is believed to be unsolvable by a classical computer.

568
01:03:40,718 --> 01:03:56,570
But importantly, while we already have these dozens of applications that we know quantum computers are going to have advantage, and also the ability to generate a lot of knowledge that can be used by AI to actually build AI models in the molecular level, if you will.

569
01:03:57,230 --> 01:04:14,410
One of the things I'm quite excited about is, as we're going to get closer to a practical quantum computer, I'm pretty confident that we're going to see many more smart people actually working on uncovering many new opportunities that we can have with quantum computing.

570
01:04:14,958 --> 01:04:27,350
So this is a new paradigm, and the new paradigm is going to create new capabilities, and many of them are actually going to be, I believe, quite dramatically improving over what we can do today with current technologies.

571
01:04:27,910 --> 01:04:30,630
And is the next paradigm, is it more about hardware?

572
01:04:31,502 --> 01:04:33,590
And is there a timeline that you see?

573
01:04:33,870 --> 01:04:35,450
Are we talking five years now?

574
01:04:36,942 --> 01:04:43,190
So we do believe that within a few years, we are going to get closer to a practical quantum computing.

575
01:04:44,654 --> 01:04:46,930
As it relates to what is it?

576
01:04:46,930 --> 01:04:53,570
I think that what we see again and again in technology is that it's really diffusion across levels.

577
01:04:54,010 --> 01:05:00,090
It's quite often the hardware is the basic on which you need to, of course, develop a software or the applications.

578
01:05:00,790 --> 01:05:07,670
But quite often, actually, by the insights you're getting from what you could do, that's actually the insights to what you want to build.

579
01:05:08,450 --> 01:05:18,590
Again, if you think about Shor's algorithm was a motivating factor to actually build more and ask the question, okay, what do we need in order to actually be able to support it?

580
01:05:19,470 --> 01:05:23,930
And then this was actually a motivation for let's see what are the problems that we need in order to do that.

581
01:05:24,622 --> 01:05:43,630
So I think we're going to see kind of this beautiful interaction across levels between problems that will matter and techniques to solve them, and what do you need in order to solve them from the algorithms perspective, from the software perspective, from the hardware perspective, and so forth.

582
01:05:44,270 --> 01:05:47,610
And that's actually a fuel, some of the fuel for the innovation for years to come.

583
01:05:48,010 --> 01:05:48,130
Right.

584
01:05:48,302 --> 01:05:53,010
And then once quantum computers become a reality, we can solve problems that are impossible today.

585
01:05:53,358 --> 01:05:59,910
And then by solving those problems, like you said, with the magic cycle, it opens up a whole new cycle of problems that we can go after and whole new innovations.

586
01:06:00,846 --> 01:06:05,830
So, clearly you are somebody that works on several decade-long projects.

587
01:06:05,934 --> 01:06:09,090
And it's rare to meet somebody that's working on just one and you work on several.

588
01:06:09,090 --> 01:06:20,030
How do you lead a team and set milestones and define progress when some of the projects also involve turning something that's actually impossible into something that's technically possible?

589
01:06:20,150 --> 01:06:21,570
How do you lead a team through that?

590
01:06:22,390 --> 01:06:31,530
Yeah, so I think the exciting aspect of looking into innovation and research is asking all the time these questions of, first, what are the things that matter?

591
01:06:31,750 --> 01:06:37,410
Where would we, should we make progress and we are in a position to make such a progress?

592
01:06:38,606 --> 01:06:41,050
Finding the right moment to actually elevate a question.

593
01:06:41,150 --> 01:06:48,290
So one question I always like to ask is, what would be even a bigger problem that you could solve in this domain?

594
01:06:48,710 --> 01:06:51,970
How can we ask, can we have the big step function?

595
01:06:52,330 --> 01:07:04,190
By the way, this is no simple measure, because what happens is that quite often when you're innovating and you're doing research, there's always the next exciting question to ask in everything that you do.

596
01:07:04,814 --> 01:07:07,930
So asking what is the big next step is non-trivial.

597
01:07:08,050 --> 01:07:09,970
This is true also for academic research, by the way.

598
01:07:11,470 --> 01:07:16,290
So you want to ask the next big question that you can actually do something about it.

599
01:07:16,390 --> 01:07:18,630
It doesn't make sense just to ask questions in random.

600
01:07:19,118 --> 01:07:28,650
So it's applying these constant questions of what would make a difference in every domain that we have and then what can we do in order to ask this next big question.

601
01:07:29,130 --> 01:07:36,550
Now Google Research has some of the best talent in the world, amazing scientists and engineers.

602
01:07:37,454 --> 01:07:43,810
We're actually, you know, have the opportunity to work on some of the most exciting problems.

603
01:07:44,010 --> 01:07:49,850
You know, we have eight products at Google that have more than 2 billion monthly active users.

604
01:07:50,710 --> 01:07:59,910
And also part of the mission is really to have the societal impact and working with partners, academic partners and governments and others, working on amazing infrastructure.

605
01:08:00,462 --> 01:08:02,090
So this is also another blessing.

606
01:08:02,090 --> 01:08:06,410
and the applications that actually reach all the people.

607
01:08:06,630 --> 01:08:21,830
So we have all these ingredients that can actually help us ask the right question and then also take whatever research breakthrough that we have and then apply them and work with amazing partners across Google in order to bring them to reality.

608
01:08:22,573 --> 01:08:32,068
What really remains a constant question to ask is what are the big next questions, research questions that could make a difference, that could have these research breakthroughs.

609
01:08:32,950 --> 01:08:43,270
Personally, what I found out again and again is that, for example, the fastest way to have innovation is to all the time have these relatively small steps towards a very big goal.

610
01:08:43,890 --> 01:08:53,250
If I go back, say, to flood forecasting, it was really iterating in every time the step is taking another step function versus what we achieved so far.

611
01:08:53,742 --> 01:08:56,729
In many cases, it required a research breakthrough in order to get there.

612
01:08:56,814 --> 01:09:05,830
Similarly with healthcare, what was given just a few years ago, now we're asking kind of bigger questions and trying to even take it to the next level.

613
01:09:06,413 --> 01:09:10,550
So this is the exciting aspect of research and innovation and technology development.

614
01:09:11,246 --> 01:09:42,649
And to me, the most exciting thing is actually to have the opportunity for us and the teams to work, not only on the, to both ask the research questions that are the state of the art and have research breakthroughs that we can actually publish in some of the most, you know, respectful venues, both anything from Nature to NeurIPS and others, to also bring that to reality and see that actually have impact on products, on science, on society.

615
01:09:42,970 --> 01:09:43,109
Right.

616
01:09:43,694 --> 01:09:52,670
And so if the first step is impossible, as it stands today, and you're telling your team of researchers, okay, this is your first milestone.

617
01:09:52,950 --> 01:09:57,450
Is it, okay, you have anywhere from six months to 10 years to get to step one?

618
01:09:57,550 --> 01:10:00,450
How do you set a benchmark on the impossible?

619
01:10:00,770 --> 01:10:10,130
So first, not all questions that we're asking belong to the impossible bucket, because in many cases, you can see, oh, I could actually make a difference here, and I could make a difference here.

620
01:10:10,798 --> 01:10:18,650
So first, we're raising all the time the bar of what is it that, because in a way, everybody's now using AI to solve problems.

621
01:10:18,650 --> 01:10:23,550
So we really want to focus on areas that are more difficult to solve in what is known today.

622
01:10:23,850 --> 01:10:25,690
So that's actually the research breakthrough.

623
01:10:26,210 --> 01:10:30,630
So it's all the time the question of, I would call it, I would say it's impact-driven.

624
01:10:31,502 --> 01:10:35,650
What would be the impact that we could have and where should we invest in order to do that?

625
01:10:36,238 --> 01:10:41,370
And where is the place that we can have the biggest impact, the biggest breakthrough?

626
01:10:42,190 --> 01:10:51,190
Typically, I think that to me, the most important questions are those actually that are going to have the step function, that are looking to the horizon, that are going to be around the corner?

627
01:10:51,662 --> 01:10:53,990
These are exciting questions from a research point of view.

628
01:10:54,530 --> 01:11:00,550
And when we achieve the research breakthrough and bring it to reality, then it's going to also have the biggest impact.

629
01:11:01,370 --> 01:11:01,750
You'll see.

630
01:11:02,090 --> 01:11:02,730
It's been a pleasure.

631
01:11:02,890 --> 01:11:03,570
Thanks so much.

632
01:11:03,750 --> 01:11:04,590
Well, thank you so much.

633
01:11:04,750 --> 01:11:05,570
Thanks for the conversation.

